        <p class="profile-detail-region">Movement · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Donghak Peasant Revolution</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeolla-province">Jeolla Province</a>, with fighting also in Chungcheong, Gyeongsang, Gyeonggi, Gangwon and Hwanghae.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Gyojo Shinwon petitions (1892–1893); the Gobu revolt (January 1894); the first revolt and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeonju">Jeonju</a> (spring 1894); rebel rule in the south (summer 1894); the second revolt and the defeat at Ugeumchi (October–November 1894); executions and mopping-up (December 1894–1895).</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Jeon Bong-jun, leader of the revolt; Kim Gae-nam and Son Hwa-jung, his generals; Choe Sihyeong, the second leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghak">Donghak</a>, who opposed the rebellion; Son Byong-hi, general of the Northern Jeob; Yi Bangeon, a Jeolla rebel leader; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-gu">Kim Gu</a>, the 'Baby Jeobju'.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The rebel armies were defeated, mainly by Japanese and government forces, and their leaders were captured and executed. Some of their demands later appeared in the Gabo Reform, and many survivors joined the Righteous Armies or the independence movement.</span></div>
        </div>
