    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The revolution began as a revolt against the abuses of Jo Byeong-gap, the magistrate of Gobu, and grew into a rising across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> whose leaders were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghak">Donghak</a> believers and whose armies were largely peasants.</li><li>After the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeonju">Jeonju</a> of May 1894, the rebels ran much of south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> through local offices called Jibgangso, until the arrival of Qing and Japanese troops and then the Japanese victory at Ugeumchi ended their hopes.</li><li>The rebel leaders were hanged in March 1895 and thousands of rebels were killed; some of their demands, such as the remarriage of widows and the freeing of enslaved people, were taken up in the Gabo Reform.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The rebels of 1894 followed rules that included not killing the innocent and paying for food in money rather than taking it, and they wore mostly white and carried bamboo spears, which gave rise to the saying &quot;When sitting, a white mountain; when standing, a bamboo mountain.&quot;</p></aside>
    </section>

